EXT. GRASS PLAINS - DUSK

A lone horse, a lone man. JEDEDIAH CROW, 60s, in a duster gone to rags, sits on a stump beside a dead fire.

He reads a letter. His lips move.

JEDEDIAH (V.O.)
Dear Hannah. I got to the river. It was dry.

He folds the page. Unfolds it. Looks at the horizon.

JEDEDIAH (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I buried the mare. Dug it with a pan.

A hawk circles. He ignores it.

JEDEDIAH (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I am coming home. It may take a while.

He tears the letter in half, then in half again. The pieces blow across the grass. He stands, dusts his knees, and whistles. The horse lifts its head.

JEDEDIAH
(quiet)
Come on.

He swings up into the saddle and turns the horse east.
